WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.110
Sir, it doesn’t work!

2
00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:05.480
I hear it twenty times a lesson.

3
00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:09.000
If I fix every program myself, my

4
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.040
pupils learn that bugs are my job.

5
00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:13.550
I want them to fix their own.

6
00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.060
Today: how to teach debugging as a skill.

7
00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:20.580
Start with the error message.

8
00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:23.370
Pupils often panic at the red

9
00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:25.190
text, but it’s a clue.

10
00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:27.310
Read the last line first:

11
00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:28.790
it names the problem.

12
00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:31.580
Then find the line number, and

13
00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.480
look there, and just above it.

14
00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:36.790
Four errors cover most beginner bugs.

15
00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:40.620
SyntaxError: Python can’t read the line,

16
00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:43.340
often a missing colon or bracket.

17
00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:46.880
NameError: a name spelt differently.

18
00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:50.300
TypeError: text and a number mixed.

19
00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:54.080
IndexError: past the end of a list.

20
00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:57.200
Then give pupils a routine.

21
00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:00.230
Read the error and find the line.

22
00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.460
Say what you expected, and what happened.

23
00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:06.050
Change one thing only.

24
00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:08.630
Run it again, and if it still

25
00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:10.610
fails, go back to step one.

26
00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:14.610
The hardest bugs give no error at all.

27
00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:17.130
The program runs, and

28
00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:18.770
prints the wrong answer.

29
00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:22.130
Here, pupils add print lines to see the

30
00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:24.610
values as it runs, or trace it on paper.

31
00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:26.780
Compare what you expected

32
00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:28.340
with what you got.

33
00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:30.240
Quick check!

34
00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:33.610
A pupil’s program stops with

35
00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:37.950
NameError: name scor isn’t defined.

36
00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.360
What is the most likely cause?

37
00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:43.510
The spelling.

38
00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:45.860
The variable was made as

39
00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:48.200
score, but used as scor.

40
00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.060
Teach pupils to compare the name in the

41
00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.180
error with the line where the variable

42
00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:55.240
was made, letter by letter.

43
00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:57.840
When a pupil calls you,

44
00:01:58.240 --> 00:01:59.300
don’t take the keyboard.

45
00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:01.300
Ask questions instead.

46
00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:02.870
What did you expect?

47
00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:05.070
What does the error say?

48
00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:07.620
What is x after line two?

49
00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:10.440
The pupil finds the bug, and

50
00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.020
learns how to find the next one.

51
00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:15.290
One more trick: rubber duck debugging.

52
00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:17.970
Pupils explain their program,

53
00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:21.350
line by line, out loud, to a

54
00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:24.210
toy, a partner or the wall.

55
00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:26.830
Halfway through, they often hear

56
00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:27.990
the mistake themselves.

57
00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:31.380
In the app, open Bug hunt.

58
00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:34.900
Six programs, six common bugs.

59
00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:38.460
Run each one before fixing it, read

60
00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:41.280
the error aloud, and notice that bug

61
00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.920
three gives no error at all: it just

62
00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:44.940
prints the wrong thing.

63
00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:46.910
Your turn.

64
00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:49.190
A pupil’s loop prints the

65
00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:50.870
wrong total, with no error.

66
00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.660
What is the best thing to say first?

67
00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:56.750
Ask what total should be.

68
00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.720
Remember: an error message is a clue; read

69
00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:02.840
the last line and the line number.

70
00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:06.350
Follow a routine: read, expect,

71
00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:08.990
change one thing, run again.

72
00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:11.820
Ask questions instead of fixing.

73
00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:15.290
And bugs with no error need tracing.

74
00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:19.540
Try it in class tomorrow: bug of the day.

75
00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:22.280
Start each lesson this week with a short

76
00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:24.800
program on the board that has one bug.

77
00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:28.290
Pupils find it in pairs, and say how

78
00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:30.450
they found it, not just what it was.
